// Detailed notes: concept id (or "term:<Term>") -> { body, example, tip }.
// Modules: Cybersecurity Fundamentals, Cybersecurity Basics, Cryptography Essentials,
// Advanced Cybersecurity & SOC, Cloud Security & Identity.
const n = (body, example, tip) => ({ body, example, tip });

export default {
  // ---------- Shared terms ----------
  'term:CIA Triad': n(
    "The CIA triad is the core model of information security. Confidentiality means only authorised people and systems can read information. It is protected by encryption, access controls, classification, and MFA. Integrity means information is accurate and has not been altered without authorisation or detection, protected by hashing, digital signatures, checksums, version control, and change management.\n\nAvailability means systems and data are accessible when needed, protected by redundancy, backups, patching, capacity planning, DDoS protection, and disaster recovery. Every security control supports at least one of the three, and every breach is a failure of at least one. The goals can conflict: stronger confidentiality controls can reduce availability, so balance them against business needs. Related concepts often added are authenticity, non-repudiation, and privacy.",
    "Ransomware attacks all three at once: it encrypts files (availability), often steals them first (confidentiality), and can tamper with backups (integrity).",
    "When an exam asks which principle a control supports: encryption = confidentiality, hashing = integrity, redundancy and backups = availability."
  ),
  'term:Zero Trust': n(
    "Zero trust is a security model built on 'never trust, always verify'. It rejects the old castle-and-moat assumption that anything inside the corporate network is trustworthy. Every access request, from any user, device, or workload, and from any location, is explicitly authenticated and authorised based on identity, device health, and context, and is encrypted.\n\nCore principles, as in NIST SP 800-207, include verifying explicitly, using least-privilege access (just-in-time and just-enough), and assuming breach, which means minimising blast radius with micro-segmentation and continuously monitoring. Enablers include strong identity with MFA and SSO, device posture checks, micro-segmentation, ZTNA instead of broad VPN access, and comprehensive logging. Zero trust is a strategy adopted incrementally, not a single product.",
    "An employee on the office network still needs MFA and a compliant, managed laptop to open the finance app, and gets access to that app only, not the whole network.",
    "The key components are the policy decision point and the policy enforcement point, which evaluate each request continuously rather than once at login."
  ),
  'term:Incident Response': n(
    "Incident response is the structured process for handling security incidents to limit damage, recover quickly, and learn. NIST SP 800-61 defines the lifecycle: Preparation (plans, playbooks, tools, contacts, training), Detection and Analysis (spotting, validating, scoping, and prioritising incidents), Containment, Eradication and Recovery, and Post-Incident Activity (lessons learned). SANS describes six steps: prepare, identify, contain, eradicate, recover, and lessons learned.\n\nContainment stops the spread, for example by isolating hosts, disabling compromised accounts, or blocking C2 domains. Eradication removes the attacker's tools and persistence and closes the entry point. Recovery restores systems from clean sources and monitors for reinfection. Throughout, preserve evidence (volatile data first, chain of custody), communicate through defined channels, and involve legal and PR for notification duties. Practise with tabletop exercises.",
    "A ransomware playbook says: isolate affected hosts from the network (but do not power them off), disable the compromised admin account, notify the IR lead and legal, and identify the initial access vector.",
    "Do not rush to wipe a compromised machine. You lose the evidence needed to find how the attacker got in."
  ),

  // ---------- Cybersecurity Fundamentals ----------
  's1-2': n(
    "Malware is any software designed to harm, exploit, or gain unauthorised access to systems. A virus attaches itself to legitimate files or programs and spreads when they are run. A worm self-replicates across networks without user action by exploiting vulnerabilities (as WannaCry did via SMB). A Trojan pretends to be legitimate software while carrying a malicious payload, often a remote access Trojan (RAT) giving attackers control.\n\nRansomware encrypts files and demands payment, often after stealing data for double extortion. Other types include spyware and keyloggers (stealing information), rootkits (hiding deep in the OS), botnets (networks of infected machines controlled remotely), fileless malware (living in memory and abusing tools such as PowerShell), and logic bombs. Defences include EDR, patching, least privilege, application allow-listing, email filtering, user awareness, and offline immutable backups.",
    "A user downloads a 'free PDF converter' that is really a Trojan; it installs a RAT, and attackers use it to deploy ransomware across the network a week later.",
    "Worms spread without user action; viruses need a host file and user execution. A frequent exam distinction."
  ),
  's1-3': n(
    "Phishing uses fraudulent emails, texts (smishing), calls (vishing), or websites to trick people into revealing credentials, approving payments, or running malware. Messages impersonate trusted brands, colleagues, or suppliers and create urgency ('your account will be locked', 'invoice overdue'). Spear phishing targets specific individuals with researched details; whaling targets executives; business email compromise (BEC) uses hijacked or look-alike accounts to redirect payments.\n\nPhishing is the most common initial attack vector. Defences are layered: email authentication (SPF, DKIM, DMARC), secure email gateways and link sandboxing, phishing-resistant MFA (FIDO2 passkeys), user training with simulations, an easy 'report phish' button, and payment-verification procedures that confirm requests through a separate channel.",
    "A finance clerk receives an email that appears to come from the CEO asking for an urgent wire transfer; a call-back verification policy stops a $250,000 loss.",
    "MFA stops most credential phishing, but adversary-in-the-middle kits can steal session cookies. FIDO2/passkeys resist even those."
  ),
  's1-4': n(
    "A Distributed Denial-of-Service attack overwhelms a target with traffic or requests from many sources, usually a botnet of compromised devices (IoT cameras and routers are common recruits), so legitimate users cannot be served. It attacks availability.\n\nTypes include volumetric attacks (floods of UDP or amplified traffic, measured in Gbps or Tbps, often using reflection and amplification through DNS, NTP, or memcached), protocol attacks (SYN floods exhausting connection tables), and application-layer (Layer 7) attacks (HTTP request floods that are hard to tell apart from real users). Defences include upstream scrubbing services, CDNs and anycast networks, cloud DDoS protection (AWS Shield, Cloudflare), rate limiting, WAF rules, auto scaling, and a pre-arranged DDoS response plan with your ISP.",
    "The 2016 Mirai botnet used hundreds of thousands of hijacked IoT devices to knock DNS provider Dyn offline, taking down major sites such as Twitter and Netflix.",
    "You cannot out-provision a large DDoS attack yourself. Absorb it upstream with a CDN or scrubbing service."
  ),
  's1-5': n(
    "In a man-in-the-middle (MITM) or on-path attack, the attacker secretly positions themselves between two communicating parties, so they can eavesdrop on, relay, or alter traffic while both sides believe they are talking directly. Techniques include ARP spoofing on LANs, rogue or 'evil twin' Wi-Fi access points, DNS spoofing, BGP hijacking, malicious proxies, and SSL stripping (downgrading HTTPS to HTTP).\n\nThe main defence is strong encryption with authentication: TLS with proper certificate validation (so attackers cannot impersonate the server), HSTS to force HTTPS, SSH host key verification, and mutual TLS between services. Network controls such as Dynamic ARP Inspection, DHCP snooping, WPA3, and 802.1X reduce opportunities, and VPNs protect users on untrusted networks such as public Wi-Fi.",
    "An attacker in a café sets up a Wi-Fi network named 'CoffeeShop_Free'. Users who connect have their unencrypted traffic captured and are shown fake login pages.",
    "Never click through certificate warnings. They are exactly what a MITM attack looks like to a user."
  ),
  's1-6': n(
    "Network defences work in layers. Firewalls enforce which traffic is allowed, by IP, port, and protocol (stateful firewalls) or by application and user (next-generation firewalls). An intrusion detection system (IDS) monitors traffic or hosts for malicious patterns (signatures) or anomalies and raises alerts. An intrusion prevention system (IPS) sits inline and blocks malicious traffic automatically.\n\nNetwork-based IDS/IPS (such as Suricata or Snort) watch traffic; host-based versions (HIDS/HIPS, often part of EDR) watch the system itself. VPNs create encrypted tunnels for remote users and between sites across untrusted networks. None is sufficient alone: defence in depth combines them with segmentation, strong identity, endpoint protection, and monitoring.",
    "An IPS inline at the internet edge blocks an exploit attempt against an unpatched web server while the IDS inside the network alerts on the internal scanning that follows a phishing compromise.",
    "IDS = detect and alert (passive); IPS = detect and block (inline). An IPS false positive can block legitimate traffic, so tune it."
  ),
  's1-7': n(
    "The OWASP Top 10 is a widely used awareness list of the most critical web application security risks, updated every few years from real-world data. The 2021 edition includes Broken Access Control (users acting outside their permissions, the number one risk), Cryptographic Failures, Injection (SQL injection and XSS, among others), Insecure Design, Security Misconfiguration, Vulnerable and Outdated Components, Identification and Authentication Failures, Software and Data Integrity Failures, Security Logging and Monitoring Failures, and Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF).\n\nIt serves as a baseline for secure development training, code review, and testing. Most entries are addressed by server-side authorisation checks, input validation and output encoding, parameterised queries, secure defaults, patched dependencies, strong authentication, and good logging. OWASP also publishes the ASVS, cheat sheets, and Top 10 lists for APIs and LLM applications.",
    "An API returns other customers' invoices when the invoice ID in the URL is changed, a classic Broken Access Control (IDOR) flaw.",
    "Broken Access Control is number one: always enforce authorisation on the server for every request, never just by hiding buttons."
  ),
  's1-8': n(
    "Multi-factor authentication requires two or more different types of factor: something you know (password or PIN), something you have (authenticator app, hardware key, smart card), and something you are (fingerprint, face). A stolen or guessed password alone is no longer enough, which stops the vast majority of account-takeover attacks. Phishing-resistant options such as FIDO2 security keys and passkeys are strongest; SMS codes are the weakest because of SIM swapping and interception.\n\nLeast privilege means every user, service account, and application gets only the permissions needed for its task, and only for as long as needed. It limits the damage a compromised account or insider can do. It is implemented with role-based access, separate admin accounts, just-in-time elevation, removal of standing admin rights, and regular access reviews.",
    "An attacker phishes an employee's password but cannot sign in without the hardware key; even if they could, the account has no admin rights to abuse.",
    "Prioritise MFA for admins, remote access, email, and cloud consoles first. These accounts give attackers the most leverage."
  ),

  // ---------- Cybersecurity Basics ----------
  'cybersecurity-basics-c2': n(
    "A threat is anything that could cause harm, such as a criminal gang, a malicious insider, or a flood. A vulnerability is a weakness it could exploit, such as unpatched software, a weak password, or an untrained employee. A risk is the likelihood and impact of a threat exploiting a vulnerability. Understanding common attack patterns helps you recognise them early.\n\nMalware infects systems to steal, spy, or destroy. Phishing tricks people into giving up credentials or running malware. Ransomware encrypts, and often steals, data to extort payment. Social engineering exploits human trust through pretexting, impersonation, tailgating into buildings, and baiting with infected USB drives. Other common attacks include password spraying and credential stuffing, exploitation of public-facing applications, and supply-chain compromises of trusted vendors.",
    "An attacker phones the helpdesk pretending to be a stressed executive locked out before a board meeting and convinces the agent to reset the password, pure social engineering.",
    "Attackers usually go for the easiest path: people and unpatched internet-facing systems. Train staff and patch exposed services first."
  ),
  'cybersecurity-basics-c3': n(
    "Authentication (AuthN) proves identity: who are you? Methods include passwords, one-time codes, certificates, hardware security keys, passkeys, and biometrics, ideally combined as MFA. Authorisation (AuthZ) happens after authentication and decides what an authenticated identity may do: which data it can read, which actions it can perform, which resources it can reach.\n\nAuthorisation models include role-based access control (RBAC, permissions assigned to roles such as 'HR Manager'), attribute-based access control (ABAC, decisions based on attributes such as department, location, and data classification), and discretionary and mandatory models. OAuth 2.0 scopes limit what applications may do on a user's behalf. Accounting or auditing, the third 'A' in AAA, logs what identities actually did.",
    "Logging into the HR portal with a password and a passkey is authentication; being allowed to view only your own team's salaries is authorisation.",
    "AuthN ≠ AuthZ. Many breaches come from apps that authenticate users correctly but never check authorisation per request."
  ),
  'cybersecurity-basics-c4': n(
    "Encryption transforms readable plaintext into unreadable ciphertext using an algorithm and a key; only holders of the right key can decrypt it. Symmetric encryption, such as AES, uses one shared secret key for both encryption and decryption. It is fast and used for bulk data. Asymmetric encryption, such as RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography, uses a public and private key pair. It solves key distribution and enables digital signatures but is slower.\n\nEncryption in transit (TLS, VPNs, SSH) protects data moving across networks; encryption at rest (full-disk encryption, database and storage encryption) protects stored data and backups if media are stolen. Key management is the hard part: generating, storing, rotating, and controlling access to keys, often with a KMS or HSM. Lose the key and the data is gone; leak the key and the encryption is worthless.",
    "A stolen laptop with BitLocker full-disk encryption exposes nothing, because the data is unreadable without the TPM-protected key and the user's PIN.",
    "Encryption protects confidentiality only if the keys are protected. Never store keys next to the data they protect."
  ),
  'cybersecurity-basics-c6': n(
    "Vulnerability management is the continuous cycle of discovering, prioritising, remediating, and verifying weaknesses in systems and software. Discovery uses authenticated vulnerability scanners (such as Nessus, Qualys, or OpenVAS), software composition analysis for application dependencies, cloud posture tools, and asset inventories, because you cannot patch what you do not know you have.\n\nPrioritisation goes beyond the CVSS severity score: consider whether the vulnerability is actively exploited (the CISA KEV catalogue, EPSS scores), whether the asset is exposed to the internet, the asset's criticality and data sensitivity, and compensating controls. Remediate by patching (tested, then deployed within agreed SLAs, for example critical internet-facing flaws within days), configuration changes, or mitigations. When fixing is not feasible, document a formal risk acceptance with an owner and an expiry date.",
    "A scanner finds 4,000 issues; the team fixes the 12 actively exploited vulnerabilities on internet-facing systems first, which removes most of the real risk.",
    "Asset inventory is step zero. Unknown, unmanaged assets are where attackers get in."
  ),
  'cybersecurity-basics-c7': n(
    "Network defences apply defence in depth so that no single failure gives an attacker full access. Firewalls enforce which traffic may pass at the perimeter and between internal zones. Segmentation, through VLANs, subnets, ACLs, and micro-segmentation, splits the network so that a compromise in one zone cannot spread freely, and it keeps sensitive systems such as payment or medical devices apart.\n\nIDS monitors traffic for malicious activity and alerts, while IPS blocks it inline. Proxies (forward proxies and secure web gateways) inspect and filter users' web traffic, blocking malicious sites and data exfiltration; reverse proxies protect servers. DNS filtering blocks known-bad domains. Network access control (802.1X) keeps unknown devices off the network, and centralised logging of all these controls feeds detection.",
    "Because the guest Wi-Fi, office, and server networks are segmented, a malware-infected guest laptop can reach the internet but nothing inside the company.",
    "Flat networks are an attacker's dream. Segmentation is one of the highest-value controls against ransomware spreading."
  ),
  'cybersecurity-basics-c8': n(
    "Secure coding means writing software that resists attack by design. Validate all input on the server (type, length, format, range) using allow-lists, and encode output for its context (HTML, JavaScript, SQL, shell) to stop injection attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting. Use parameterised queries or safe ORM features instead of building SQL strings. Avoid dangerous functions and insecure deserialisation of untrusted data, and in memory-unsafe languages prevent buffer overflows.\n\nRely on well-reviewed libraries for authentication, session management, and cryptography rather than writing your own. Enforce authorisation on every request, handle errors without leaking details, keep secrets out of code, and keep dependencies patched. Build security into the development lifecycle with threat modelling, code review, SAST, DAST, and dependency scanning in CI.",
    "Replacing `\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name='\" + name + \"'\"` with a parameterised query closes a SQL injection hole that allowed dumping the whole user table.",
    "Never trust client-side validation alone. Attackers bypass the browser and call your API directly."
  ),
  'cybersecurity-basics-c10': n(
    "Security governance is written down in documents with different levels of authority. Policies are high-level management statements of intent and requirements ('all remote access requires MFA'). Standards specify mandatory technical requirements ('use FIDO2 or authenticator-app MFA; SMS is not permitted'). Procedures are step-by-step instructions, and guidelines are recommended but optional practices.\n\nFrameworks provide tested baselines: the NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 (Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover), the CIS Critical Security Controls (prioritised technical safeguards), ISO/IEC 27001 (a certifiable information security management system), and MITRE ATT&CK (a knowledge base of adversary techniques for building detections). Regulations such as GDPR, HIPAA, and PCI DSS impose legal or contractual obligations. Compliance shows controls exist at audit time; security maturity shows they work every day.",
    "A company maps its controls to CIS Controls v8 to prioritise improvements, pursues ISO 27001 certification for customers, and uses ATT&CK to test detection coverage.",
    "Compliant does not mean secure. Many breached organisations passed their last audit."
  ),

  // ---------- Cryptography Essentials ----------
  's2-1': n(
    "Symmetric encryption uses the same secret key to encrypt and decrypt. It is very fast, even in hardware (CPUs include AES instructions), so it is used for bulk data: full-disk encryption, database and file encryption, VPN tunnels, and the data phase of every TLS connection. AES (with 128- or 256-bit keys) is the standard; ChaCha20 is a fast alternative on devices without AES hardware.\n\nModes of operation matter. Authenticated encryption modes such as AES-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305 provide confidentiality and integrity together, while ECB mode leaks patterns and must never be used. The central challenge is key distribution: both parties need the same secret without exposing it, which is why asymmetric cryptography or key exchange is used to agree on symmetric keys.",
    "BitLocker and FileVault encrypt entire drives with AES; the AES key is itself protected by the TPM or the user's password.",
    "Symmetric = one shared key, fast, bulk data. Weakness: securely sharing the key. Legacy algorithms such as DES, 3DES, and RC4 are broken or deprecated."
  ),
  's2-2': n(
    "Asymmetric (public-key) cryptography uses a mathematically linked key pair. The public key can be shared with anyone; the private key must stay secret. Data encrypted with the public key can be decrypted only with the private key, which gives confidentiality to the key owner. A signature made with the private key can be verified by anyone with the public key, which gives authentication, integrity, and non-repudiation.\n\nCommon algorithms are RSA (2048-bit or larger) and elliptic-curve cryptography (ECDSA, Ed25519, ECDH), which offers equivalent security with much smaller keys. Asymmetric operations are far slower than symmetric ones, so in practice they are used to exchange or protect symmetric keys and to sign data, not to encrypt bulk data. This hybrid approach is exactly how TLS, PGP, and S/MIME work. Post-quantum algorithms such as ML-KEM and ML-DSA are being adopted to resist future quantum attacks.",
    "To send Bob a secret, Alice encrypts it with Bob's public key; only Bob's private key can decrypt it. To prove she sent it, Alice signs it with her own private key.",
    "Encrypt with the recipient's public key; sign with your own private key. Mixing these up is the classic exam trap."
  ),
  's2-3': n(
    "A cryptographic hash function turns input of any size into a fixed-size digest (SHA-256 produces 256 bits) with three key properties: it is one-way (you cannot recover the input from the hash), deterministic (the same input always gives the same hash), and collision-resistant (it is infeasible to find two inputs with the same hash). A tiny change to the input produces a completely different hash, the avalanche effect.\n\nHashes verify integrity (download checksums, file integrity monitoring, blockchain), underpin digital signatures and HMACs (hash plus secret key, for message authentication), and are used for password storage. For passwords, use deliberately slow, salted algorithms such as Argon2id, bcrypt, scrypt, or PBKDF2, because fast hashes such as SHA-256 can be brute-forced at billions of guesses per second on GPUs. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for security use.",
    "A software vendor publishes the SHA-256 hash of its installer; if your download's hash matches, the file was not corrupted or tampered with in transit.",
    "Hashing is not encryption. It cannot be reversed with a key. Hashing supports integrity, not confidentiality."
  ),
  's2-4': n(
    "Transport Layer Security (the successor to the deprecated SSL) protects data in transit between a client and a server, providing confidentiality (encryption), integrity (tamper detection), and server authentication (certificates). HTTPS is HTTP over TLS, and TLS also secures email transport, VPNs, APIs, and database connections.\n\nIn the handshake, the client and server agree on a protocol version and cipher suite, the server presents its certificate for the client to validate (chain of trust, host name, and expiry), and both sides perform an ephemeral key exchange (ECDHE) to derive shared session keys, which provides forward secrecy. TLS 1.3 simplified this to one round trip and removed weak algorithms. Use TLS 1.2 or 1.3 only; SSL 2.0/3.0 and TLS 1.0/1.1 are deprecated.",
    "A browser connecting to your bank validates the bank's certificate, negotiates TLS 1.3 with an AES-GCM cipher, and shows the padlock. An expired certificate would trigger a warning.",
    "Forward secrecy (ephemeral Diffie-Hellman) means stealing the server's private key later cannot decrypt previously recorded sessions."
  ),
  's2-5': n(
    "Public Key Infrastructure is the system of roles, policies, and technology that binds public keys to identities. A Certificate Authority (CA) verifies an identity and issues an X.509 certificate containing the subject's name (and Subject Alternative Names), public key, validity period, and issuer, signed with the CA's private key. Trust flows through a chain: a trusted root CA (preinstalled in operating systems and browsers) signs intermediate CAs, which sign end-entity certificates.\n\nCertificates expire and can be revoked before expiry if a key is compromised, with revocation checked through CRLs or OCSP (often with OCSP stapling). Registration Authorities handle identity verification. Organisations also run private internal CAs, for example AD Certificate Services, for internal services and devices. Let's Encrypt issues free, automated 90-day certificates via the ACME protocol, making automated renewal the norm.",
    "A website's certificate chain: example.com, signed by an intermediate CA, signed by a root CA that the browser already trusts.",
    "Certificate expiry is a leading cause of outages. Automate renewal (ACME) and monitor expiry dates."
  ),
  's2-6': n(
    "A digital signature proves who created data and that it has not changed since. The signer hashes the data and signs that hash with their private key. A verifier hashes the data themselves and uses the signer's public key to check the signature; if the check passes, the data came from the private-key holder (authentication), was not altered (integrity), and the signer cannot credibly deny signing it (non-repudiation).\n\nSignatures secure software updates and code signing, operating-system drivers and bootloaders (Secure Boot), TLS certificates, documents and contracts, emails (S/MIME, DKIM), container images (Sigstore/cosign), and blockchain transactions. Algorithms include RSA-PSS, ECDSA, and Ed25519. A signature is only as trustworthy as the protection of the private key and the certificate binding the public key to an identity.",
    "An operating system installs a driver update only if it carries a valid signature from the vendor, blocking tampered or fake drivers.",
    "Signing provides integrity, authentication, and non-repudiation, but not confidentiality. Signed data can still be read by anyone."
  ),
  's2-7': n(
    "Diffie-Hellman key exchange lets two parties who have never met agree on a shared secret over a public, monitored channel. Each side generates a private value and sends a public value derived from it; combining their own private value with the other's public value gives both the same secret, while an eavesdropper seeing only the public values cannot feasibly compute it. The shared secret then becomes the key for fast symmetric encryption.\n\nModern systems use ephemeral elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDHE), generating fresh values for every session. This provides perfect forward secrecy: compromising a server's long-term private key later does not expose past sessions. Plain Diffie-Hellman does not authenticate the parties, so it is combined with certificates and signatures to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks, as in TLS and SSH.",
    "Every TLS 1.3 connection performs an ECDHE exchange, so even an attacker who records traffic and later steals the server key cannot decrypt it.",
    "Diffie-Hellman provides key agreement, not authentication. Without signatures or certificates it is vulnerable to MITM."
  ),
  's2-8': n(
    "Passwords must never be stored in plaintext or with reversible encryption. Instead, store a salted, slow hash. A salt is a unique random value generated per password and stored alongside the hash. It ensures identical passwords produce different hashes and makes precomputed rainbow tables useless.\n\nThe hash function must be deliberately slow and ideally memory-hard, so each guess is expensive: Argon2id (the current recommendation), bcrypt, scrypt, or PBKDF2 with a high iteration count, tuned so one check takes tens to hundreds of milliseconds. A pepper, a secret key stored separately from the database, adds protection if only the database leaks. Combine this with breached-password checks, rate limiting, and MFA. When a breach happens, attackers get hashes that are expensive to crack rather than usable passwords.",
    "Two users both choose 'Summer2026!'; with unique salts their stored bcrypt hashes look completely different, so cracking one does not reveal the other.",
    "Fast hashes (MD5, SHA-1, even plain SHA-256) are wrong for passwords. Use Argon2id or bcrypt."
  ),

  // ---------- Advanced Cybersecurity & SOC ----------
  's3-1': n(
    "A penetration test is an authorised, simulated attack against defined systems to find and demonstrate exploitable weaknesses before real attackers do. It begins with scoping and rules of engagement: written permission, targets in and out of scope, testing windows, allowed techniques, and emergency contacts. Testing without authorisation is illegal.\n\nTypical phases are reconnaissance, scanning and enumeration, exploitation, post-exploitation (privilege escalation, lateral movement, and showing business impact), and reporting. Knowledge levels vary: black box (no inside information, simulating an outsider), white box (full access to documentation and code), and grey box (partial knowledge, such as a user account). The deliverable is a report with an executive summary, findings rated by risk, evidence, and clear remediation guidance, followed by a retest to confirm fixes.",
    "A grey-box test starting with a standard employee account reaches Domain Admin in two days through a misconfigured service account, a finding that drives a tiering project.",
    "No written authorisation = no test. Get sign-off from someone with the authority to approve it."
  ),
  's3-2': n(
    "Ethical hacking uses the same knowledge, tools, and techniques as malicious attackers, but with permission and to improve security. Ethical hackers work within a defined scope and rules of engagement, avoid causing damage, protect any data they encounter, and report findings responsibly to the organisation. Roles include penetration testers, red teamers, bug bounty hunters, and security researchers.\n\nThe discipline requires broad skills (networking, operating systems, web applications, cloud, Active Directory, scripting) and constant learning as new vulnerabilities and techniques appear. Certifications include CEH, OSCP, and PNPT, and legal practice environments include TryHackMe, Hack The Box, and PortSwigger's Web Security Academy. Responsible disclosure and coordinated vulnerability disclosure programmes govern how researchers report flaws they find.",
    "A bug bounty researcher finds an access-control flaw in an airline's booking API, reports it through the official programme, and is paid a reward once it is fixed.",
    "Stay inside scope. Testing a system you were not authorised to test is illegal even with good intentions."
  ),
  's3-3': n(
    "Nmap is the standard network scanner for discovering hosts, open ports, running services and versions (-sV), and operating systems (-O), with the Nmap Scripting Engine adding vulnerability checks. Metasploit Framework is a modular exploitation platform: pick an exploit for a known vulnerability, pair it with a payload (such as Meterpreter), set options, and run it, plus post-exploitation modules for privilege escalation and pivoting.\n\nBurp Suite is the leading web application testing proxy. It intercepts and modifies HTTP traffic between the browser and the application, and provides a scanner, Repeater (manually resend and tweak requests), Intruder (automated fuzzing), and many extensions for finding injection, authentication, access-control, and business-logic flaws. OWASP ZAP is a free alternative. Other essentials include Wireshark, Hashcat and John the Ripper, BloodHound, and Impacket.",
    "A tester uses Nmap to find an outdated web server, Burp Suite to discover an IDOR flaw in its API, and Metasploit to demonstrate remote code execution on an unpatched service.",
    "Defenders should know these tools too. Their traffic patterns and artifacts are what your detections must catch."
  ),
  's3-4': n(
    "A Security Operations Center is the team, processes, and technology that monitor an organisation's environment around the clock and respond to threats. Analysts work from a SIEM (centralised log correlation and alerting), EDR and XDR (endpoint and extended detection and response), network detection, email security, and threat intelligence, and often use SOAR (security orchestration, automation and response) to automate repetitive enrichment and containment steps.\n\nWork is often tiered: Tier 1 triages alerts, dismissing false positives and escalating real ones; Tier 2 investigates incidents in depth and contains them; Tier 3 handles complex incidents, threat hunting, malware analysis, and detection engineering. Key metrics include mean time to detect (MTTD) and mean time to respond (MTTR). Alert fatigue is a major challenge, addressed by tuning detections and automation. Some organisations outsource this to managed detection and response (MDR) providers.",
    "At 2 a.m. the SIEM correlates an impossible-travel sign-in with a new mailbox forwarding rule; a Tier 1 analyst escalates, and Tier 2 disables the account and revokes its sessions within 15 minutes.",
    "Detections are only valuable if someone acts on them. Tune noisy rules, because alert fatigue hides real attacks."
  ),
  's3-5': n(
    "Threat hunting is the proactive search for attackers who have evaded automated detection. Instead of waiting for alerts, hunters assume compromise and form hypotheses based on threat intelligence, MITRE ATT&CK techniques, or anomalies, then query telemetry (EDR data, logs, network flows, DNS) to confirm or refute them.\n\nExamples of hypotheses: 'An attacker is using scheduled tasks for persistence', 'Service accounts are logging in interactively', or 'A host is beaconing to a newly registered domain at regular intervals'. Techniques include stacking (finding rare values across the fleet), baselining normal behaviour, and pivoting on indicators. Successful hunts either find intrusions or become new automated detections, so every hunt improves future coverage.",
    "A hunt for rare parent-child process pairs finds Excel spawning PowerShell on three machines, revealing a malicious macro campaign that no alert had caught.",
    "Hunting needs good telemetry. You cannot hunt in data you are not collecting."
  ),
  's3-6': n(
    "Incident response is how an organisation handles a confirmed or suspected security breach. The lifecycle runs from preparation (IR plan, playbooks, roles, contact lists, tools, retainers) through detection and analysis (confirming, scoping, and prioritising the incident), containment (stopping the spread by isolating hosts, disabling accounts, and blocking indicators), eradication (removing malware, persistence, and the root-cause vulnerability), and recovery (restoring from clean backups and monitoring closely), to lessons learned.\n\nSpeed limits damage, but evidence matters too: collect volatile data first (memory, network connections), maintain chain of custody, and keep a detailed timeline. Communication runs through defined channels, out of band if email may be compromised, with legal counsel involved early because of breach-notification laws (such as GDPR's 72-hour rule) and regulators. Rehearse with tabletop exercises and technical drills.",
    "During a ransomware incident, the team isolates infected segments, preserves memory images of patient-zero machines, restores from immutable backups, and notifies regulators within the legal deadline.",
    "Prepare before you need it: decide in advance who can authorise shutting down systems, and keep offline copies of the IR plan."
  ),
  's3-7': n(
    "A red team emulates a real adversary. It is often objective-based ('access the payment database without being detected') and uses stealth, social engineering, physical intrusion, and custom tooling over weeks, testing the organisation's ability to detect and respond rather than just finding vulnerabilities. A blue team is the defence: SOC analysts, incident responders, and engineers who monitor, detect, and respond.\n\nPurple teaming brings them together: red executes specific techniques (often mapped to MITRE ATT&CK), while blue watches detections in real time; gaps are fixed immediately and retested. This collaborative loop improves detection coverage much faster than adversarial exercises alone. Tools such as Atomic Red Team, CALDERA, and breach-and-attack simulation platforms help automate technique testing.",
    "A red team phishes its way in and moves laterally for a week unnoticed; the follow-up purple-team sessions build detections for each technique it used.",
    "Pentest = find as many vulnerabilities as possible in scope. Red team = test detection and response against a realistic adversary."
  ),
  's3-8': n(
    "Zero trust architecture, formalised in NIST SP 800-207, removes implicit trust based on network location. Every access request is evaluated by a policy engine using identity (strong authentication), device posture (managed, patched, EDR healthy), resource sensitivity, and context (location, time, behaviour), and enforced by policy enforcement points in front of resources. Access is least privilege and time-limited, sessions are continuously re-evaluated, and all traffic is encrypted and logged.\n\nPractical building blocks include an identity provider with MFA and conditional access, device management, ZTNA replacing broad VPN access, micro-segmentation for workloads, and strong monitoring with analytics. Zero trust is especially suited to cloud, SaaS, and remote work, where the traditional network perimeter no longer exists. It is adopted as a journey, starting with identity and the most critical assets.",
    "A developer on home Wi-Fi reaches the production console only through a ZTNA gateway after passkey MFA, from a compliant company laptop, with a session that expires after one hour.",
    "Zero trust assumes breach. Design so that one compromised device or account gives access to as little as possible."
  ),

  // ---------- Cloud Security & Identity ----------
  'sec-1': n(
    "Cloud Identity and Access Management controls who (a principal: user, group, role, or service identity) can perform which actions on which resources, under which conditions. In AWS, JSON policies allow or deny actions (s3:GetObject) on resources (specific ARNs). Every API call, from the console, CLI, or SDK, is authenticated and then evaluated against the applicable identity policies, resource policies, permission boundaries, session policies, and organisation-level controls (service control policies).\n\nEvaluation logic in AWS starts from an implicit deny; an explicit allow is required, and any explicit deny overrides all allows. Best practices include using roles with temporary credentials instead of long-lived access keys, federating human access through SSO, requiring MFA, granting least privilege, using conditions (such as source IP, MFA present, or tags), and continuously reviewing permissions. Misconfigured IAM, meaning over-broad permissions and leaked keys, is a leading cause of cloud breaches.",
    "An EC2 instance uses an IAM role granting read access to only one S3 bucket, so there are no stored access keys to leak and no access to any other data.",
    "An explicit Deny always wins in AWS policy evaluation. Use SCP guardrails to deny dangerous actions organisation-wide."
  ),
  'sec-2': n(
    "Least privilege means each identity, whether human, service account, application, or pipeline, receives only the permissions required to do its job, scoped to specific resources and, ideally, only for the time needed. It shrinks the blast radius when credentials are stolen or misused and limits insider risk.\n\nIn practice: start from no access and add specific permissions; avoid wildcards such as \"Action\": \"*\" and \"Resource\": \"*\"; separate duties (the person who writes code is not the one approving production access); use just-in-time elevation for admin tasks instead of standing admin rights; and continuously right-size using access analysis tools (such as AWS IAM Access Analyzer, last-accessed data, and CIEM products) that show which permissions are actually used. Review access regularly, especially when people change roles or leave.",
    "Access analysis shows a CI pipeline role with AdministratorAccess only ever uses 11 actions; restricting it to those removes a huge risk if the pipeline is compromised.",
    "Permissions tend to grow over time (privilege creep). Schedule periodic access reviews and remove unused rights."
  ),
  'sec-4': n(
    "Encryption at rest protects data stored in databases, block volumes, object storage, file systems, snapshots, and backups, so that stolen, leaked, or improperly disposed storage media are unreadable without the keys. Cloud services typically use AES-256 with envelope encryption: data is encrypted with a data key, and that data key is itself encrypted by a master key held in a key management service.\n\nMost cloud services now enable encryption at rest by default. The meaningful decision is who controls the keys: provider-managed keys (simplest), customer-managed keys in KMS (you control the key policy, rotation, and auditing, and can disable the key to cut off access), or customer-supplied or external keys held in your own HSM for the strictest requirements. Encryption at rest does not protect against an attacker who gains authorised access through the application, since the service decrypts data for authorised callers.",
    "An S3 bucket encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key denies reads to anyone whose role lacks kms:Decrypt on that key, even if the bucket policy were misconfigured.",
    "Encryption at rest protects against media theft and some misconfigurations, but not against compromised valid credentials."
  ),
  'sec-5': n(
    "Encryption in transit protects data moving over networks, between users and services, between services, and to databases and APIs, from eavesdropping and tampering. The main tool is TLS (1.2 or 1.3), used for HTTPS, database connections, message queues, and internal service calls. Mutual TLS (mTLS), where both client and server present certificates, adds strong service-to-service authentication, often provided automatically by a service mesh.\n\nEncrypt in transit everywhere, including inside VPCs and private networks, because zero trust assumes internal networks can be compromised. Enforce HTTPS with HSTS and redirects, require TLS on database and cache connections, disable old protocols and weak ciphers, manage certificates automatically (for example with AWS Certificate Manager or cert-manager), and use IPsec or MACsec for network-level encryption where needed.",
    "A bank enables TLS between its application pods and the database cluster, so an attacker who compromises one host on the subnet cannot sniff customer data.",
    "'It's inside our VPC' is not a reason to skip TLS. Internal traffic is a common target for lateral-movement sniffing."
  ),
  'sec-6': n(
    "A Key Management Service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault, Google Cloud KMS) creates, stores, and controls cryptographic keys inside hardware security modules that never expose raw key material. Services and applications call the KMS to encrypt, decrypt, or generate data keys (envelope encryption), and every use is authorised by key policies and IAM and logged, for example to CloudTrail, which gives a full audit trail of who used which key.\n\nKey features include automatic rotation of key material, separation between key administrators and key users, grants for temporary delegated use, multi-region keys, disabling or scheduling deletion of keys (which effectively crypto-shreds all data encrypted with them), and import of your own key material. Protect key administration tightly, because whoever controls the keys controls the data.",
    "An application encrypts customer documents with data keys from KMS; auditors review CloudTrail logs showing exactly which service role decrypted which documents and when.",
    "Separate duties: people who manage keys should not automatically be able to use them to decrypt data, and vice versa."
  ),
  'sec-7': n(
    "The shared responsibility model divides security duties between the cloud provider and the customer. The provider is responsible for security of the cloud: physical data centres, hardware, the hypervisor and host infrastructure, and the global network. The customer is responsible for security in the cloud: their data, identities and access, configurations, and whatever software they manage.\n\nThe split depends on the service model. With IaaS (VMs), the customer manages the guest OS, patching, applications, firewall rules, and data. With PaaS (managed databases, serverless), the provider also handles the OS and runtime, while the customer manages configuration, access, and data. With SaaS, the provider runs almost everything, but the customer still owns user access, data, and settings. Most cloud breaches, such as public storage buckets, leaked keys, and over-permissive IAM, fall on the customer's side.",
    "AWS patches the hypervisor under your EC2 instance, but if you never patch the instance's Linux OS and it gets exploited, that is your responsibility.",
    "In every model, data classification, identity and access management, and configuration remain the customer's responsibility."
  ),
  'sec-8': n(
    "Multi-factor authentication requires a user to present two or more independent factors (knowledge, possession, inherence) before access is granted. In the cloud it is essential because consoles and APIs are reachable from anywhere on the internet. MFA should be mandatory for the root or global administrator account, all privileged roles, and increasingly for every user, typically enforced centrally at the identity provider with conditional access policies.\n\nStrength varies. FIDO2 security keys and passkeys are phishing-resistant, because they are cryptographically bound to the real site. Authenticator app codes and push notifications are good but can be phished or abused through 'MFA fatigue' prompt bombing, which number matching mitigates. SMS and voice codes are the weakest, vulnerable to SIM swapping. Protect the account recovery process too, since it can bypass MFA entirely.",
    "Attackers with a leaked root-account password are stopped by the hardware security key required for every root sign-in.",
    "Lock the root account away: strong MFA, no access keys, and use it only for the few tasks that truly require it."
  ),
  'sec-9': n(
    "Single sign-on lets users authenticate once with a central identity provider (IdP), such as Microsoft Entra ID, Okta, or Google Workspace, and then access many applications without signing in again. Applications (service providers) trust the IdP through federation standards: SAML 2.0 (XML assertions, common for enterprise SaaS) and OpenID Connect (built on OAuth 2.0 with JSON Web Tokens, common for modern and mobile apps).\n\nSSO improves usability (one strong credential instead of dozens of reused passwords) and security: MFA and conditional access are enforced in one place, access is logged centrally, and disabling one account cuts off every connected app at once, which is crucial when people leave. The trade-off is that the IdP becomes a high-value target, so protect it with strong MFA, admin separation, and monitoring. SCIM automates provisioning and deprovisioning of accounts into apps.",
    "When an employee leaves, disabling their single Entra ID account instantly revokes access to Salesforce, Slack, AWS, and 40 other SSO-connected apps.",
    "SAML = XML assertions for enterprise SSO; OIDC = identity layer on OAuth 2.0; OAuth 2.0 itself is for authorisation (delegated access), not authentication."
  ),
  'sec-10': n(
    "Compliance means demonstrating that security and privacy controls meet an external standard, regulation, or contract. Common frameworks include SOC 2 (independent attestation of controls for security, availability, confidentiality, processing integrity, and privacy; Type II covers a period of operation), ISO/IEC 27001 (a certifiable information security management system), PCI DSS (payment card data), HIPAA (US healthcare information), GDPR (EU personal data), and FedRAMP (US government cloud).\n\nCloud providers hold many certifications, and customers can inherit controls for the provider's side (physical security, infrastructure) through provider reports such as AWS Artifact. The customer must still configure and evidence their own side: access control, encryption, logging, data residency, and incident response. Continuous compliance tooling (such as AWS Config, Azure Policy, and CSPM products) monitors configurations against these frameworks between audits.",
    "A SaaS startup uses its cloud provider's SOC 2 report for infrastructure controls, and implements and evidences its own access reviews, encryption, and logging to pass its own SOC 2 Type II audit.",
    "Using a compliant cloud provider does not make your application compliant. Your configuration is still in scope."
  ),
};
